Subject: GUNS: Buy Chinese (was: Don't buy Chinese)

Simon Levy:

>I would like to discourage people on this list from buying guns, or 
> any other products, made in mainland China.

I would like to encourage people on this list to buy guns, or 
any other products, made in mainland China (or anywhere else).

>  As we remember from the student uprisings in Beijing, China has one
> of the most repressive governments on earth.

That's not how I remember it.

The students created the denouement through their own political naivete.
When Premier Li visited T-square to talk to the students about their
concerns they spat on him.  Spittle not words.  Constructive attitude,
eh?  The students, IMO, backed the authorities into a situation where
they felt they had to respond in force or lose face.  The hysterical
reaction by Western governments and public showed an amazing lack of
insight that surprised even me - on par with the folly of the students,
I guess.  (note: I'm not condoning what the authorities did, but their 
reaction was to a large extent forced by the insulting behaviour of the
students and should have come as no surprise.)

The true agent of reform in China is the market.  The hand of central
control has been steadily relaxing over the past 2 decades and an
increasing proportion of the economy is under private control (there
have been a number of articles in the Economist in the past couple of
years that have hard data on the steady shift from public to private).
To encourage economic reform (a necessary prerequisite for political 
reform - see S.Korea) we should by _more_ mainland Chinese goods, not 
less.

