Date: Mon, 20 Sep 93 06:43:33 GMT
From: price@price.demon.co.uk (Michael Clive Price)
Subject: Meaninglessness

I wrote:

>>Can you formulate an operational test of "existence exists"?  No.
>>Therefore it is a meaningless utterance.

T. David Burns responded:

> To say that  "existence exists" is non-operational may be accurate.

Glad we're agreed on this.  :-)

> To say that it is stupid, or wrong, or a waste of time may give us
> some information. But to say that it's meaningless exhibits 
> incredible gall... and mammoth myopia ....

On contrary, it is a statement of how to test for meaningful content. 
An example from physics is the old notion of absolute space and motion. 
Once upon a time it was thought that Jerusalem was the centre of the
universe.  But what did that mean?  Could you perform an experiment that
would confirm this?  Or even say what it meant, apart from putting big
J at the centre of maps?  No, so the notion was dropped.  Same with
absolute motion.  When scientists realised that no experiment could
measure motion relative to some "at rest" state they abandoned the
concept.

It's also how I distinguish concepts.  Concepts that are operationally
indistinguishable are identified with each other.  Eg 'free-will', that
bugbear of philosophy, is operationally indistinguishable from 'will'. 
Hence we are justified in dropping the 'free-' prefix, since it is a
meaningless adjunct.  Try it and see - life is so much simpler with the
operational pruning knife!

> Unlike philosophers, who can safely spend their entire lives
> innocent of any but the most naive notions of 'hard science,' 
> 'hard scientists' have to live in a human world  of meaning.

Which is why they have to have clear notions of what constitutes
meaningful and meaningless statements.

> Trying to redefine meaning to please one's self will not do the 
> trick.

"My" notion of meaning, defined operationally, happens to be one that
has proved successful in science.  What's good enough for Newton and
Einstein is good enough for me (there we are, a blatant, unabashed
appeal to authority!).  I'll stick with it until someone comes up with
something better.

Mike Price                         price@price.demon.co.uk

