From: more@chaph.usc.edu (Max More)
Subject: Realism & Idealism: Bishop Berkeley

In the process of catching up on the last 20 or so Digests, I noticed
someone claiming that the philosopher Bishop Berkeley thought that 
nothing exists.  The poster (sorry, don't remember who) said that
Berkeley could be refuted by kicking any object.

This is, of course, incorrect, and the purported disproof is actually one
proferred by by a contemporary of Berkeley's.  To be fair to the Bishop,
his claim was that there are no such things as physical objects, if this
is taken to mean objects existing independently of any mind.  He thought
he had good reason to believe that "physical objects" were orderly collections 
of mind-dependent ideas.  To avoid the counter-intuitive result that objects
exist only while being perceived (and the result that the apparently identical
object I'm looking at must be a different object from the one I'm
looking at), he declared that objects continue to always be perceived by God.

Berkeley's view may sound ridiculous, but it was really a logical result GIVEN
the dominant representationalist metaphysics of the time.  Certainly his
view was more internally consistent that that of John Locke.  Only David Hume
went further in consistency.

Certainly I disagree with Berkeley, but he wasn't a complete fool, given his
historical and philosophical context.  Kelley's much-mentioned book,
the Evidence of the Senses, is about as good a piece of philosophy as I've
seen from an Objectivist (which can't be taken as especially high praise).
I think he errs in some ways, such as trying to maintain the strict
Objectivist distinction between sensation and perception (defended here by
Tim Starr), but he makes some good arguments in favor of direct realism (and 
not the naive form some posters seem to think constitutes the Objectivist
view).

Upward and Outward!

