This is just a restatement of my point. You posit reading through all the  
laws (!) concerning postal delivery looking for a prohibition. This is proof  
by positive evidence. You aren't looking for the absence of something, you  
are looking for the prohibition - the purple rhino.

Perry offered me a grand if I could show that such a law *did not* exist.  
Even though the set of all laws pertaining to postal delivery is finite, it  
would be unwieldy to prove this negative claim. There are a couple problems.  
The first is that I could simply lie and say "I looked and found none, pay  
up". If Perry insists on "proof" then we run into the second problem - I will  
have to present him with the full body of postal law. He will then have to  
confirm that I have provided him with *all* such laws, and then he will have  
to read it to see that it does in fact contain no prohibition. Obviously,  
this is a unacceptable way to approach the problem. It makes much more sense  
for the wager to be phrased in such a way that a person has an incentive to  
find rhino, not hide it. Offering someone a grand to find the prohibition is  
much more likely to produce results, even if that result is "I couldn't find  
anything". Note that that result doesn't close the question, but neither does  
it reward someone for an ambiguous, or inaccurate search.

Final note - Perry has not as yet provided me with the wager specifics I  
requested. Until he does so, I will not undertake to search the body of law  
to establish the point. And as far as I'm concerned, until I or someone else  
does, the question I raised - "Does the government actually enforce a letter  
delivery monopoly through force of law" will remain unanswered. I am sure  
many on the list will choose to believe it does. I myself, if forced to  
guess, would say it does. But taking things on faith is not becoming to a  
group of people who apparently pride themselves (perhaps overmuch) with a  
rational and consistent adherence to the truth.

